# {{name}}

An [eieio](https://github.com/tlugger/eieio) block: a WASM module a node loads, configures
and wires into a service.

It reads each incoming signal's `n` and emits it incremented. That behaviour is a starting
point; the shape around it is the part worth keeping.

## The shape

```
src/lib.rs           the block: one struct, one #[block] attribute, one Block impl
tests/native.rs      the fast layer — the block as native Rust, no engine (SDK §6.1)
conformance/         the boundary layer — the same block as .wasm, under the reference
                     harness (ABI §13.1)
.cargo/config.toml   the shadow-stack size, which decides how much linear memory this
                     block declares (SDK §5.2)
```

Both test layers exist because neither catches the other's bugs. `tests/native.rs` runs in
milliseconds and fails with a Rust backtrace; the conformance scenarios are the only place
linear memory, `(ptr, len)` and CBOR crossing an engine are exercised at all. A scenario is
a **data file**, so the same one runs on every host — including the leaf runtime.

## Working on it

```sh
cargo eio test      # native tests, then the module, then the conformance scenarios
cargo eio build     # the module and its manifest.json
```

`cargo eio build` builds for `wasm32-unknown-unknown` with `panic = "abort"` and the size
profile (SDK §5.2), then runs the same load-time validation a node runs — exports and their
signatures, imports within the declared capabilities, the embedded manifest — and writes
that manifest as `manifest.json` beside the `.wasm`. A module that builds is a module a node
accepts.

It also prints the module's declared minimum linear memory. One page, 64 KiB, is what this
template produces and what a leaf-class node can instantiate (LEAF §4.2); the shadow stack
in `.cargo/config.toml` is the lever, and raising it is fine as long as you know the block
is not headed for a leaf.

## Configuring it

Every property is an expression (ABI §11). `next` defaults to `(+ $n 1)`, and whoever deploys
this block may replace it without rebuilding — which is also how to try one out:

```sh
eio-daemon dev run-block target/wasm32-unknown-unknown/release/{{lib}}.wasm \
  --prop 'next=(* $n 2)' --batch '[{"n": 21}]'
```

Expressions are pure and terminating: no IO, no clock, no RNG, no loops. `$n` on a signal
that has no `n` is an error rather than a null — a config typo fails loudly and per signal,
not silently at 2 a.m.

## Adding a port, a property, a capability

All three are attribute arguments on the struct, and all three reach the manifest from
there — there is no second place to keep in step:

```rust
#[block(
    name = "{{name}}",
    inputs(r#in),
    outputs(out, rejected),      // Out::Rejected now exists; emitting elsewhere will not compile
    capabilities(state, timer)   // ctx.state() and ctx.timers() now exist
)]
```

A capability you declare and never call is a module every conformant host refuses (ABI §4.2),
so declare what you use.
